The bug is that `appendQuotedString(..., escapingOn=true)` can return without advancing `pos`.  
In `applyPattern`, that means the outer `while (pos.getIndex() < pattern.length())` loop revisits the same quote forever, repeatedly appending until `OutOfMemoryError`.

The patch fixes exactly that root cause:

```java
int start = pos.getIndex();
char[] c = pattern.toCharArray();
next(pos);
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

Now the early-return path always consumes the quote character before returning, so the caller cannot loop forever on the same position.

I do not see a remaining input that still reaches the same fault:
- `applyPattern(..., escapingOn=true)` no longer leaves `pos` unchanged on a quote.
- The same is true if `appendTo == null`; `pos` is still advanced.
- Other paths in `appendQuotedString` already advance `pos` via `next(pos)` in the loop.

So this is not merely test-specific; it removes the underlying non-advancing-position bug that caused the infinite loop / OOM.

