The original failure is caused by `appendQuotedString(...)` not advancing `pos` when it sees an escaped quote at the current position and `escapingOn == true`:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

At the `applyPattern(...)` call site, that means the outer `while` loop sees the same quote again and again, repeatedly appending until memory is exhausted. That matches the observed `OutOfMemoryError`.

The patch changes that behavior to:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    pos.setIndex(pos.getIndex() + 1);
}
```

This removes the root cause: the parse position now always advances past the opening quote instead of returning immediately with `pos` unchanged. So the outer loop can no longer get stuck on the same character, which was the mechanism behind the OOM.

I do not see a concrete input that still triggers the same non-advancing-position / infinite-growth fault after this change. The method may have awkward semantics, but the specific bug is fixed at its source rather than special-cased only for the test input.

